{:edb/db-before-t 2143 :edb/db-after-t 2144 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535456 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:30:20.696Z" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087542 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087542 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186087542 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1, -2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-2 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x}, \\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9 \\alpha e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9αe^{3x} + 3αe^{3x} - 2αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\nα(9 + 3 - 2)e^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10αe^{3x} &= 20e^{3x} \\\\\n10α &= 20 \\\\\nα &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + 2e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 false] [17592186087542 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1, -2,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9  \\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha (9+3-2){e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is \n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087543 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4652a17e8836f9ea84df971537f3c0df1f5ccd2b2dfd7d8d9615ef69a775b972.png)" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087543 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 3y = e ^ {x}$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186087543 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1,-3\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{-3 x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{x}$, and $e^{x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} = (1 + x)αe^{x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (2 + x)αe^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 + x)αe^{x} + (1 + x)αe^{x} - 2xαe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n(2 + x + 1 + x - 2x)αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3αe^{x} &= e^{x} \\\\\n3α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 13 x e ^x\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Be^{-2x} + \\frac{1}{3}xe^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 false] [17592186087543 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/bb796871deb9ebb6ff0ac3c98715a74275b8957374753a1bfed998898de136f8.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/fbd5028588b1b3639ae465a5904a49e354da5f77ae0224b398666449dcfd809b.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/4d6d063a30dc8f4bab52eab0764e8dee217ddd74055d79cae2bcca34e46ca45f.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14065386/assets/df86a6554579076b0783330da9971ee6ac847ce0d7615c81da959cc197372021.png) is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture