{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087544 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186087544 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 3\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x}$, and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy_{p}}{dx} &= (3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x}, \\frac{d^{2}y_{p}}{dx^{2}} = (9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2)αe^{3x} - 6(3x^{2} + 2x)αe^{3x} + 9αx^{2}e^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n(9x^{2} + 12x + 2 - 18x^{2} - 12x + 9x^{2})αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2αe^{3x} &= e^{3x} \\\\\n2α &= 1 \\\\\nα &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{c} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 false] [17592186087544 1145 "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root $\\lambda = 3,$ the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}-6(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+9\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+12x+2-18{x}^{2}-12x+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =1 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087553 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\nAe^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = 1.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087553 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}$\n\nhas the general solution\n$Ae^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2, \\ \\ y'(0) = 1$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186087553 1145 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{3x}+3Bx{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+3B(0){e}^{0}+0{e}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =3A+B \\\\ 1 & =3(2)+B \\\\ -5 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2{e}^{3x}-5x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186087553 1145 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n$2|= Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} + \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 2|= A$.\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n$y^{′} = 3Ae^{3x} + Be^{3x} + 3Bxe^{3x} + xe^{3x} + \\frac{3}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}$,\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n$1|= 3Ae^{0} + Be^{0} + 3B(0)e^{0} + 0e^{0} + \\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}e^{0}; 1|= 3A + B; 1|= 3(2) + B; - 5|= B$.\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5$, we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny = 2e^{3x} - 5xe^{3x} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}e^{3x}\n$$" 13194139535456 false] [17592186087808 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350115 13194139535456 true] [17592186087808 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350143 13194139535456 true] [17592186087808 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350157 13194139535456 true] [17592186087808 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350164 13194139535456 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350122 13194139535456 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350171 13194139535456 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350178 13194139535456 true] [17592186087809 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350185 13194139535456 true] [17592186087810 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350129 13194139535456 true] [17592186087810 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350136 13194139535456 true] [17592186087810 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350192 13194139535456 true] [17592186087810 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350199 13194139535456 true] [17592186087810 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350206 13194139535456 true] [17592186087811 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350150 13194139535456 true] [17592186087811 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350213 13194139535456 true] [17592186087811 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350220 13194139535456 true] [17592186087811 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350227 13194139535456 true] [17592186088172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535456 true] [17592186088172 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4y = e^{-3x}$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x} + Be^{-4x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535456 false] [17592186088172 1100 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139535456 true] [17592186088172 1145 "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is ${e}^{-3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+3(-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ (9-9-4)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186088333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535456 true] [17592186088333 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186088333 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 12y = 10e^{2x}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186088333 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-12(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-2-12){e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+4B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3A+4B-2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A+4B=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=1.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186088394 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535456 true] [17592186088394 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139535456 true] [17592186088394 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 4e^{3x}$." 13194139535456 false] [17592186088394 1145 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\